  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Little change to the forecast reasoning and\nsynoptic setups through Thursday. An amplified upper trough \nwill be shifting east out of the Central U.S. A progressive and\nopen wave will sweep through the Eastern U.S at the end of the \nweek. Before that happens, low level flow is currently veering \nsoutheast to south as weak coastal trough lifts through, which \nis producing some widely scattered showers from Swanquarter to\nJacksonville and along the southern coast. Another fog and low \nstratus threat tonight into Thursday morning as moistening \nboundary layer continues. Southerly flow Thursday, but with no \nforcing in place will maintain PoPs generally less than 20%. \nWarm Thursday with highs in the lower 80s interior to 70s \nbeaches. Overnight lows remain above climatology, with readings\nin the 60s the next few nights.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Friday - Still looks like widespread showers \nand a few thunderstorms breaking out during the afternoon into \nevening as a cold front moves through Eastern NC. Anomalous \nmoisture and higher-than-normal instability for early November \nis expected, as indicated by EFI highlighting CAPE vals nearing \nthe 80th percentile. Have maintained thunder threat for inland \nareas where the EFI is highlighting the higher CAPE/Shear \nindices inland, and where highest instability and shear will \nreside. If best forcing lines up with peak heating, a strong to \nsevere thunderstorm threat would materialize for areas\nespecially west of HWY 17 Friday afternoon. Main threat would \nbe damaging wind gusts. The front sweeps offshore late Friday \nwith any remaining showers exiting the OBX before daybreak Sat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Good agreement amongst models with frontal\npassage late Friday, allowing a more seasonably dry airmass to\nusher in for the weekend. Return to mostly sunny skies and \nhighs in the upper 60s to low 70s Sat, then cooler yet for Sun \nwith 60-65. Lows will be in the 40s, with some 30s for some of \nthe normally colder spots.",
